<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Roman Cyrenaica</h1><p class="st-meta">74 BCE – 643 CE · capital Cyrene</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Roman and Byzantine province of eastern Libya, a Greek-speaking land of five cities on the Jebel Akhdar.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a> was bequeathed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-apion">Ptolemy Apion</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ptolemaic-kingdom-of-cyrene">Ptolemaic Kingdom of Cyrene</a> in 96 BCE and organised as a province in 74 BCE. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-creticus">Quintus Metellus</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> in 67 BCE, the two were joined, by the time of Augustus, as the province of Crete and Cyrenaica, administered by a proconsul.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Greek cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pentapolis-cyrenaica">Pentapolis</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-cyrene">Cyrene</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollonia-cyrenaica">Apollonia</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/barca">Ptolemais</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taucheira">Taucheira</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/euesperides">Berenice</a>, kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-language">Greek language</a> and institutions. The region exported grain and wine, though silphium died out under Roman rule. A large Jewish community rose in the revolt of 115 to 117 CE, devastating Cyrene. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> split the region into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> Superior and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya-inferior">Libya Inferior</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/synesius">Bishop Synesius of Ptolemais</a> described raids by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/austuriani">Austuriani</a> around 400.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From 395 the province belonged to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, but its cities declined. In 642 to 643 Arab forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amr-ibn-al-as">Amr ibn al-As</a> took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/barca">Barca</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pentapolis-cyrenaica">Pentapolis</a> for the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a>, and the region became an Islamic province known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Barqa</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/roman-cyrenaica.jpg" alt="Roman Cyrenaica" width="320" height="267"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Empire_-_Creta_et_Cyrenaica_(125_AD).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>74 BCE – 643 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cyrene</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman religion, Judaism, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Libya</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Crete and Cyrenaica, Libya Superior, Libya Pentapolis</dd></dl></aside></div>
